## Keymap presets (parity plan C7, decision D11, lane P12): which key runs
## which command. `classic` is today's key table; `vscode` follows the keys
## people and agents already know from VS Code. The editor still starts on
## `classic` until the designer confirms the flip (D11). Keys are specs as
## `edit.ail` writes them: modifiers in the order ctrl, alt, shift, then the
## key in lower case (`ctrl+shift+p`, `alt+left`, `f5`). On macOS the host
## maps Command to ctrl, so a preset writes `ctrl+` for both. Chords such as
## Ctrl-K Ctrl-C are not supported; every binding is one key press.

## One key of a preset: pressing `key` runs the command named `command`, as
## the palette names it (`KeyRow.name` in `edit.ail`).
pub struct Binding
    pub command str
    pub key str

## One row of the README key table: the keys as the table shows them for
## `classic`, the command's palette name ("" for rows that are no command,
## such as the arrows) and what it does.
pub struct Row
    pub keys str
    pub name str
    pub does str

## Commands `classic` binds that `vscode` leaves to the palette: their
## classic key is a well-known VS Code key of another command.
pub PALETTE_ONLY_IN_VSCODE := ["Next theme", "Join lines"]

## The palette-only commands of the key table (rows with a name and no key)
## on this base, so a test can check that every preset names real commands.
pub PALETTE_COMMANDS := [
    "Select all occurrences",
